{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.95,"content_digest":"f687a11c8d0d139f53a7ed226cc42a95e2d639f7de57d2455313c6b9a18f54f8","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:25:36+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:399d8734ca5c520a467584fa","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:interdisciplinary-science:01M0HE1EV1B03NK9RB41BX68D0","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The NP-hardness of computing L(G) on connected bipartite graphs with maximum degree three and a perfect matching is proven by reduction from a known NP-hard problem. The reduction-completeness meta-principle directly supports the conclusion that the target problem is at least as hard as the source problem.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:25:36+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.95,"content_digest":"69643f8c739b93e0d5babd27d22751dcf14403595aab48a399ce73435b3a3d7e","created_at":"2026-08-22T18:41:41+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"8c36b294b471ae10d411a691552410926b79aa5c5f4eaf152f3cb2222d8ec710","input_tokens":5177,"latency_ms":19071,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"e7aefba62267e3b1c92e1da10f2611acc8fc5c4d4d6c0bbd5e485ec443ccf7bd","output_tokens":1770,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:91015bf56ce5e9c547655e48","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NCE2KWFDRDQGRCRDVQJJGJ","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle states that an efficient reduction from a hard problem A to problem B transfers hardness to B. The MAXCON theorem is exactly such a case: a fixed-parameter reduction from k-CLIQUE, which is W[1]-hard, to MAXCON-D with d=k establishes W[1]-hardness of MAXCON with respect to k. The reduction type, answer preservation, and parameterized hardness scale are compatible with the meta-principle's scope.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:06:05+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T18:41:41+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
